(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-inflammatory-lung-disease-vignettes"]={"title":"Inflammatory Lung Diseases Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 47-year-old man with cardiac sarcoidosis has corticosteroid-refractory disease. He also has rheumatoid arthritis, and methotrexate he once took for it was stopped after it was implicated in lung injury. His team is weighing methotrexate, azathioprine, infliximab and continued corticosteroids. Which of these should be avoided?","choices":["Continued corticosteroids","Methotrexate","Infliximab","Azathioprine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Methotrexate is one of the immunosuppressants used for corticosteroid-refractory sarcoidosis, but it has also been implicated as a cause of interstitial lung disease, and in him it already was. Azathioprine or infliximab covers the same refractory indication without that history. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 18, 46, 47"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has 8 months of progressive exertional dyspnea and a persistent nonproductive cough, with bibasilar dry rales and small lung volumes on examination. Which combination of total lung capacity and ratio of forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity is expected?","choices":["High capacity, low ratio","Normal capacity, low ratio","Normal capacity, normal ratio","Low capacity, normal or high ratio"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Interstitial lung disease stiffens the lung so it no longer expands, giving a restrictive pattern: total lung capacity and forced vital capacity fall while the ratio of forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity stays normal or rises. Her small lung volumes on examination fit. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 23, 24, 26, 29"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman who has cut and polished stone for 22 years has silicosis. Her clinician reviews the known complications of silicosis, one of which is a connective tissue disease. Which connective tissue disease is it?","choices":["Scleroderma","Ankylosing spondylitis","Polymyositis-dermatomyositis","Sjögren's syndrome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The complications of silicosis are an increased risk of tuberculosis, progressive massive fibrosis and scleroderma. Scleroderma is also one of the connective tissue diseases that produce interstitial lung disease, so new findings in her deserve that thought. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 25, 64"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with exertional dyspnea and bibasilar crackles has pulmonary function testing: forced vital capacity 58% of predicted (normal 80% or more), total lung capacity 61% of predicted (normal 80% or more), and a ratio of forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity of 0.86 (normal 0.70 or more). How is this pattern classified?","choices":["Mixed pattern","Restrictive pattern","Obstructive pattern","Normal study"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A restrictive pattern is a normal or increased ratio of forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity with decreased total lung capacity and forced vital capacity. Her ratio of 0.86 is preserved while both capacities sit near 60% of predicted, the pattern interstitial lung disease produces. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 29, 34"},{"q":"A 66-year-old retired construction worker has asbestosis with exertional dyspnea and fine bibasilar crackles. He asks about a procedure to wash the asbestos fibers out of his lungs. Which treatment plan fits?","choices":["Corticosteroids to dissolve the fibers","Methotrexate to stop the fibrosis","Oxygen, physiotherapy and vaccinations","Lavage to wash out the fibers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of asbestosis is supportive because asbestos fibers invade lung tissue and cannot be removed. Oxygen therapy, physiotherapy, medications and vaccinations make up that care, while the scarring itself comes from chronic inflammation and macrophage invasion forming fibrosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 5, 18, 46, 56, 62"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with sarcoidosis has a chest film showing diffuse reticular infiltrates throughout both lungs. Her previously enlarged hilar nodes are no longer seen, and there is no honeycombing or hilar retraction. Which radiographic stage is this?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stage III is parenchymal involvement alone, seen as diffuse reticular infiltrates, with no hilar adenopathy. Her film has infiltrates without enlarged nodes and without the honeycombing, hilar retraction, bullae or cysts that would mark stage IV. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 57-year-old stone worker has had silicosis for 8 years. He asks which complication his lung disease puts him at increased risk of. Which is it?","choices":["Hypercalcemia","Goodpasture syndrome","Tuberculosis","Mesothelioma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Silicosis carries an increased risk of tuberculosis, together with progressive massive fibrosis and scleroderma. That is why patients with silicosis should have a tuberculosis skin test and a current chest film, and why the risk deserves mention at follow-up. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 25, 44, 59, 64"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man with severe pancreatitis develops profound dyspnea on hospital day 2. His chest film shows new bilateral opacities not explained by effusion, atelectasis or nodules, and an echocardiogram shows normal cardiac function. His arterial oxygen pressure is 62 mm Hg on a fraction of inspired oxygen of 0.40. How is his oxygenation impairment classified?","choices":["Oxygenation criterion not met","Mild acute respiratory distress syndrome","Moderate acute respiratory distress syndrome","Severe acute respiratory distress syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Divide arterial oxygen pressure by fraction of inspired oxygen: 62 / 0.40 = 155 mm Hg. That is under the 300 threshold and falls between 100 and 200, the moderate class. Severe pancreatitis is a systemic trigger, and his new opacities and normal heart complete the criteria. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 72, 73"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis quit smoking at diagnosis. She asks whether any medicine has been shown to help her live longer. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Corticosteroids reverse the fibrosis","Methotrexate slows the scarring","Surgical biopsy removes the disease","No treatment has improved survival"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Management of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is controversial: nothing has been shown to improve survival or quality of life compared with no treatment. Accurate diagnosis helps identify patients who may benefit from available therapies, and her smoking cessation remains critical to prolonging life. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 5, 18, 35, 37, 38"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man is found to have interstitial lung disease on computed tomography after months of exertional dyspnea and dry cough. No cause has been identified yet. Which part of his history looks for the most common group of known causes?","choices":["Work and environmental exposures","Recent pneumonia or bronchitis","Medicines and radiation treatment","Age and sex at symptom onset"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The most common interstitial lung diseases of known etiology come from occupational or environmental exposures: inorganic dusts, organic dusts, and fumes or gases. Asking about his work and surroundings looks for agents such as asbestos fibers, silica dust, or moldy hay and bird excreta. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 12, 13, 32, 55, 60, 65"},{"q":"A 59-year-old former shipyard worker with asbestosis smokes a pack of cigarettes a day. He asks what his cigarettes add to his asbestos exposure. Which risk do they markedly increase?","choices":["Lung carcinoma","Tuberculosis","Hypercalcemia","Progressive massive fibrosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In asbestos workers, cigarette smoking increases the prevalence of radiographic pleural and parenchymal changes and markedly increases the incidence of lung carcinoma. Asbestosis already carries an increased risk of lung cancer, especially in a smoker. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 44, 54, 55, 56, 64"},{"q":"A 66-year-old retired coal miner with coal workers' pneumoconiosis and chronic dyspnea asks what can be done for his lungs. He already uses home oxygen and stopped smoking years ago. Which measure belongs in his care?","choices":["Early prone positioning","Pneumococcal, influenza vaccines","A curative antifibrotic drug","Avoiding moldy hay and birds"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Coal workers' pneumoconiosis has no effective curative treatment, so care is supportive: oxygen, pneumococcal and influenza vaccinations, rehabilitation and smoking cessation, with lung transplant for severe cases. With oxygen in place and smoking stopped, vaccination is the supportive measure still to add. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 54, 71, 74"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man is recovering after 3 weeks in the intensive care unit for acute respiratory distress syndrome. He has muscle wasting, weakness and persistent fatigue. Which referral addresses these problems?","choices":["Yearly ophthalmologic evaluation","Physical rehabilitation","Lung transplant evaluation","Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Long-term recovery after acute respiratory distress syndrome includes treating physical sequelae: post-intensive care muscle wasting, weakness and persistent fatigue. Rehabilitation addresses them while his lung function continues to improve over 6 to 12 months. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 48, 54, 71, 74"},{"q":"A 55-year-old farmer with farmer's lung has stopped handling moldy hay, but he remains hypoxic with cyanosis, tachypnea and tachycardia, and his disease is judged severe. Which treatment may be helpful now?","choices":["Early prone positioning","High-frequency oscillation","Return to work with a mask","Corticosteroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. After the agent is identified and avoided, corticosteroids may be helpful in severe cases of hypersensitivity pneumonitis, with other immunosuppressants and supportive care such as oxygen. His persistent hypoxia, cyanosis, tachypnea and tachycardia despite avoidance mark severe disease. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 65, 68, 69, 71, 74"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who still uses a pack of cigarettes a day is diagnosed with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. Which intervention is critical to prolong his life?","choices":["Oral corticosteroids","Avoiding an inhaled antigen","Methotrexate","Smoking cessation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Smoking is the cause in the majority of cases, 67 to 75% of patients with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis have a smoking history, and cessation is critical to prolong life. Management is otherwise controversial, because nothing has improved survival or quality of life compared with no treatment. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 31, 37, 38, 46, 71"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man did demolition work around asbestos insulation for 20 years. He now has a pleural effusion, and mesothelioma is suspected. Which test can establish the diagnosis?","choices":["Pleural fluid cytology","Bronchoalveolar lavage","Tuberculosis skin test","Serum antinuclear antibody"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mesothelioma, a primarily pleural cancer nearly always caused by asbestos exposure, frequently produces pleural effusions, and it can be diagnosed from pleural fluid cytology. His long asbestos exposure and new effusion make the fluid the sample to examine. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 25, 56, 59, 62, 64"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis has taken methotrexate for 6 years. She now has progressive exertional dyspnea, a dry cough, bibasilar inspiratory crackles and a restrictive pattern on pulmonary function testing. She asks whether her arthritis medicine could be involved. Which response is accurate?","choices":["No, drug injury needs cancer chemotherapy","Yes, methotrexate deposits dust in the lungs","Yes, methotrexate can cause this disease","No, methotrexate protects the lungs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Methotrexate is used for many autoimmune diseases, including lupus and rheumatoid arthritis, and it has also been implicated as a cause of interstitial lung disease. Rheumatoid arthritis is itself a connective tissue cause, so her disease and her treatment both belong in the differential. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 18, 50, 60"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has 2 months of mild exertional dyspnea, and high-resolution computed tomography shows early interstitial lung disease. Her lung examination is normal, and she asks whether that means her lungs are healthy. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Early disease can have a normal exam","A normal exam rules out lung disease","Crackles appear before scan changes","Clubbing is the first sign to appear"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Early in interstitial lung disease the physical examination may be normal; small lung volumes, bibasilar dry rales or inspiratory crackles and distal clubbing appear as the disease develops. Her scan already shows the disease, so a normal examination does not mean healthy lungs. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 24, 57"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man is resuscitated after a near-drowning. Within 24 hours he has refractory hypoxemia and new bilateral opacities on chest film with a normal heart size, and an echocardiogram shows normal cardiac function. On the ventilator his arterial oxygen pressure is 54 mm Hg on a fraction of inspired oxygen of 0.60. How is his oxygenation impairment classified?","choices":["Oxygenation criterion not met","Mild acute respiratory distress syndrome","Moderate acute respiratory distress syndrome","Severe acute respiratory distress syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Divide arterial oxygen pressure by fraction of inspired oxygen: 54 / 0.60 = 90 mm Hg. A ratio under 100 is the severe class. Near-drowning is a direct pulmonary insult, and new bilateral opacities within a day with a normal heart complete the criteria. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 72, 73"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with newly diagnosed interstitial lung disease asks how the inflammation in his lungs turns into scar tissue. Which sequence is accurate?","choices":["Alveolitis, then fibroblasts, then collagen","Collagen, then fibroblasts, then alveolitis","Fibroblasts, then alveolitis, then collagen","Alveolitis, then collagen, then fibroblasts"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages, lymphocytes, monocytes and neutrophils accumulate in the alveolar walls, producing alveolitis. Alveolitis leads to fibroblast production, and the collagen the fibroblasts produce causes the fibrosis: permanent scar tissue that stiffens the lung and impairs gas exchange. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 5, 6"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has bilateral hilar adenopathy on chest film and sarcoidosis is suspected, but his clinical and radiographic findings also raise the possibility of lymphoma. What is the next step?","choices":["Repeat chest film in a year","Tissue biopsy","Oral corticosteroids","Serum angiotensin-converting enzyme level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy is essential whenever clinical and radiographic findings suggest an alternative diagnosis such as lymphoma. Transbronchial lung or fine-needle node biopsy also confirms sarcoidosis itself by showing noncaseating granulomas, so tissue settles the question either way. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 44, 45, 47, 48"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman develops profound dyspnea and new bilateral opacities 36 hours after multiple blood transfusions for a gastrointestinal bleed. She has a history of heart failure. What is the next step before acute respiratory distress syndrome can be diagnosed?","choices":["Echocardiogram","Routine pulmonary artery catheter","Pulmonary function testing","Serum antibodies to an inhaled agent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis requires respiratory failure not fully explained by heart failure or fluid overload, and echocardiographic assessment of cardiac function excludes hydrostatic cardiogenic pulmonary edema. Multiple transfusions are a recognized trigger, but her heart failure history makes that exclusion essential. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 69, 72, 73, 74"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman admitted with sepsis develops profound dyspnea 30 hours later. She has tachypnea, intercostal retractions and crackles, and her hypoxemia does not improve with supplemental oxygen. Her chest film shows new bilateral opacities with a normal heart size, and an echocardiogram shows normal cardiac function. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Cardiogenic pulmonary edema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. About one-third of cases present with sepsis, and profound dyspnea develops 12-48 hours after the insult. New bilateral opacities within 7 days, hypoxemia refractory to supplemental oxygen and a normal heart on film and echocardiogram fit this non-cardiogenic syndrome. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 33, 35, 72, 73"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with a lung contusion from a motor vehicle crash develops new bilateral opacities on hospital day 3 that are not explained by effusion, atelectasis or nodules. An echocardiogram shows normal cardiac function. Her arterial oxygen pressure is 75 mm Hg on a fraction of inspired oxygen of 0.30. How is her oxygenation impairment classified?","choices":["Oxygenation criterion not met","Mild acute respiratory distress syndrome","Moderate acute respiratory distress syndrome","Severe acute respiratory distress syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Divide arterial oxygen pressure by fraction of inspired oxygen: 75 / 0.30 = 250 mm Hg, which is under the 300 threshold and inside the 200-300 range of the mild class. Lung contusion is a direct pulmonary insult, and new bilateral opacities within 7 days with normal cardiac function complete the criteria. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 72, 73"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman with biopsy-proven sarcoidosis has bilateral hilar adenopathy alone on chest film and no eye, heart or nervous system involvement. Her serum calcium is 11.9 mg/dL (normal 8.5-10.5). Which treatment is indicated?","choices":["Observation with yearly films","Inhaled bronchodilators","Oral corticosteroids","Methotrexate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her calcium of 11.9 mg/dL is above the 8.5-10.5 range, and hypercalcemia is one of the indications for oral corticosteroids in sarcoidosis. About 90% of cases respond to corticosteroids, so they come first even when the chest film shows hilar adenopathy alone. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 44, 46, 47, 48, 71"},{"q":"A 56-year-old coal miner has severe complicated coal workers' pneumoconiosis with worsening dyspnea despite oxygen, vaccinations, pulmonary rehabilitation and smoking cessation. Which referral may be indicated?","choices":["Lung transplant evaluation","Thoracic surgery to remove dust","Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation","Yearly ophthalmologic evaluation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management of coal workers' pneumoconiosis is supportive, with oxygen, vaccinations, rehabilitation and smoking cessation, and in severe cases lung transplant may be indicated. He has severe disease that is worsening despite every supportive measure on the list. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 48, 54, 74"},{"q":"A 41-year-old cheese maker has recurrent fever, chills, cough and dyspnea hours after handling moldy cheese at work. Hypersensitivity pneumonitis is diagnosed, and her symptoms are mild. What is the first step in treatment?","choices":["Oral corticosteroids","Lung transplant evaluation","Inhaled bronchodilators alone","Identify and avoid the agent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of hypersensitivity pneumonitis starts by identifying the agent responsible and avoiding it. Prompt diagnosis is key to removing the patient from the irritating environment: symptoms can be reversible if caught early, while continued exposure may worsen the disease. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 65, 71"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with a 40 pack-year smoking history has 18 months of insidious dry cough, exertional dyspnea and fatigue. He has clubbing, bibasilar inspiratory crackles, tachypnea and reduced chest expansion. Computed tomography shows diffuse, patchy fibrosis with pleural-based honeycombing. He has no occupational exposures, medicines or connective tissue disease. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Asbestosis","Sarcoidosis","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Insidious dry cough, exertional dyspnea, fatigue, clubbing, bibasilar inspiratory crackles, tachypnea and reduced chest expansion are the picture of idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia, and pleural-based honeycombing with no associated cause is idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. Most patients have a smoking history. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 33, 34, 35, 40, 55, 58, 65"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman received chest radiation for breast cancer 3 years ago. She now has interstitial lung disease with a restrictive pattern and has no occupational exposures. She asks whether her cancer treatment could be the cause. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Yes, by acting as an inhaled dust","Yes, radiation is a recognized cause","No, radiation affects the skin alone","No, cancer treatment spares the lungs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Radiation is one of the treatment-related causes of interstitial lung disease of known cause, listed with methotrexate, amiodarone, nitrofurantoin and chemotherapeutics. It is also named among the causes that provoke an inflammatory and fibrotic lung response, so her chest radiation is a credible cause. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 13, 17"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has taken amiodarone for atrial fibrillation for 6 years. Over several months he has developed progressive exertional dyspnea, a dry cough and bibasilar inspiratory crackles, and pulmonary function testing shows a restrictive pattern. He has no occupational exposures. How is his interstitial lung disease classified?","choices":["Known cause, occupational exposure","Granulomatous lung disease","Known cause, treatment related","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone sits with radiation, methotrexate, nitrofurantoin and chemotherapeutics in the treatment-related group of interstitial lung disease of known cause. His exertional dyspnea, dry cough, crackles and restrictive pattern fit interstitial disease, and his years of amiodarone supply the cause. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 20, 23, 35"}]};
